@inherits Umbraco.Cms.Web.Common.Views.UmbracoViewPage
@using System.Globalization
@*
    Dictionary lookups in views. Replace the keys with the site's real item set.

    - In Razor, Umbraco sets CurrentUICulture per request from the matched domain (else the
      default language), so the parameterless overloads already follow the visitor's culture.
    - Always use the OrDefault variant (or a language fallback) where a missing key must not
      blank the UI - GetDictionaryValue returns "" for an absent key.
    - CultureDictionary is Umbraco's CACHED dictionary - safe on the render path; never call
      IDictionaryItemService here.

    Multi-value property editors: a Textstring property whose VALUE is a dictionary key lets
    editors pick a label without typing text - render it with
    GetDictionaryValue(Model.Value<string>("myProperty")). See "Translating Multi-Value
    Property Editors" in
    https://docs.umbraco.com/umbraco-cms/develop-with-umbraco/tutorials/multilanguage-setup.md
*@

@* Current request culture, with a fallback if the key is missing/untranslated. *@
<span id="dict-read-more">@Umbraco.GetDictionaryValueOrDefault("Site.ReadMore", "Read more")</span>

@* Explicit culture overload - normally you let the request culture drive this instead. *@
<span id="dict-search-da">@Umbraco.GetDictionaryValue("Site.Search", new CultureInfo("da-DK"))</span>

@* Missing key: falls back instead of rendering "". *@
<span id="dict-missing">@Umbraco.GetDictionaryValueOrDefault("Site.DoesNotExist", "fallback text")</span>

@* Grouped labels: every direct child of the "Site" parent item, for the request culture. *@
<ul id="dict-site-children">
    @foreach (var label in Umbraco.CultureDictionary.GetChildren("Site"))
    {
        <li data-key="@label.Key">@label.Value</li>
    }
</ul>
